# L §4.2: "A day in added may be a hole (HOLE-CALENDAR where it decides a result) or a window (one_of([…]),
# window(a, b)): every day known but one. Such a window is one the line meets, so a result that depends on its day is
# HOLE-IMPRECISE unless the line states windows each (§4.4)"; L §8.1 HOLE-IMPRECISE: "a read depends on where in a
# window the day falls and the line states no windows each: ... a window in a calendar's added list", message "date
# within <window> not precise enough for <comparison or use>". 2026-03-03 (a Tuesday) lies in the window: that hole;
# 2026-03-05 (a Thursday) lies outside it: a business day; 2026-03-07 is a Saturday: no business day whatever the added
# day is; one_of likewise for 2026-03-10. (Writer 6: was REFCORE-UNSPECIFIED §4.2. Writer 8, fifth pass, W-B-Q1: the
# question was L §8.1 HOLE-CALENDAR's "calendar <c> adds a day within <window>: is it <d>?", a row L §8.1 no longer
# has.)
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
input len: calendar
input len2: calendar

version v
  cite s
  governs 2026
  known 2025-12-01
  len = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], added [window(2026-03-02, 2026-03-04)])
  len2 = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], added [one_of([2026-03-10, 2026-03-12])])

ask q1: is_business(2026-03-03, len), is_business(2026-03-05, len), is_business(2026-03-07, len)
ask q2: is_business(2026-03-10, len2), is_business(2026-03-11, len2)
=== expect
q1: (?"date within window(2026-03-02, 2026-03-04) not precise enough for is_business(2026-03-03, len)" (len), true, false)
  cut 2026-12-31
q2: (?"date within one_of([2026-03-10, 2026-03-12]) not precise enough for is_business(2026-03-10, len2)" (len2), true)
  cut 2026-12-31
